  "summary": "Stacked lacquer pillboxes on a silk cord — miniature cabinetmaking worn at the waist.",
  "description": "The inro is a stack of two-to-five sealed compartments strung on a silk cord and held by a carved netsuke toggle, born because Edo formal dress had no pockets: samurai and merchants carried seals, medicines and perfumes in these lacquered minicabinets. Makers — Shibata Zeshin the most celebrated — layered roiro urushi black lacquer with maki-e gold dust, shell inlay and takamakie relief, turning daily objects into jewel boxes. Together with ojime beads, the ensemble remains the summit of Japanese small-work luxury.",
